WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:03.610
Hey, David, you talk about undecided voters.

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:09.769
Who the hell could possibly be undecided right
now about Trump versus Biden?

00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:11.130
I agree with the sentiment.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:16.500
I've said before almost verbatim, who the
hell could be undecided right now?

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.040
Only if you've not been paying attention.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.040
Right.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.160
Could you be undecided at this point.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:26.250
And that indeed is the case if you look at
who are number one swing voters.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.800
And number two, who is undecided right now.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:36.920
What you find is that the undecideds and swing
voters tend to be younger.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.519
Centrists to center right.

00:00:39.519 --> 00:00:41.470
Less engaged in politics.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.559
They aren't actually paying attention.

00:00:43.559 --> 00:00:50.530
So, for example, 72 percent of voters who
are 65 or older have decided for sure they

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:55.109
know who they're going to vote for among 18
to 29 year olds.

00:00:55.109 --> 00:00:57.460
Only 47 percent were decided.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.449
As of a few weeks ago.

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:01.309
That's crazy.

00:01:01.309 --> 00:01:07.150
But it makes sense in from the standpoint
of if you don't know that much about what's

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.970
going on, you're more likely to say, I don't
know who I'm going to vote for or whether

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:12.970
I'm going to vote.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:15.790
It is people who are paying less attention.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:18.640
It is people who are less informed.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.180
If you are remotely informed and you are a
likely voter.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:26.590
You voted before or you're sure that you're
going to vote.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:28.680
It's tough to be undecided right now.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:36.390
We've had almost four years now of impossibly
dramatic differences between what Donald Trump

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:42.320
has done and how he has governed and what
a Joe Biden presidency would mean to the United

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:43.430
States.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:51.530
Now, the other question about undecideds is
a lot of undecideds don't think it's that

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:55.850
important whether one candidate or the other
candidate wins.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:05.520
In other words, when you ask all voters whether
it matters who wins, 82 percent to 92 percent

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.700
say that it matters.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.840
When you ask these swing and undecided voters.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.569
Does it matter who wins?

00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:14.420
Only two thirds believe that it matters.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:19.960
So you start to put together a picture of
the undecided voter tends to be younger.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:23.540
Ideologically, they are moderate to center
right.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:28.030
They aren't paying much attention and they
don't think that the quiet consequences actually

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:29.820
matter that much.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.160
That makes sense.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:35.060
If you fall under those categories, it follows
logically.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:40.480
I don't agree with those points of view, but
it follows logically that you might say may

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.850
me maybe, maybe Trump, maybe Biden.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:48.680
Those who are politically engaged have overwhelmingly
been decided for a long time.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:54.600
Those who have voted in the past have been
overwhelmingly decided for a long time.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.150
And in terms of issues.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:02.030
One other interesting tidbit is that people
who are still undecided today or as of about

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:09.849
two weeks ago, not only are they more uninformed,
they tend to vote based on their gut feeling

00:03:09.849 --> 00:03:15.209
of how the economy is doing and their gut
feeling more generally.

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:23.950
That tracks, sadly, with someone who could
be bamboozled, essentially tricked into voting

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.040
for Donald Trump.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.310
And that's what's very, very scary.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:32.200
And why I am repeating and hammering on and
harping on.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:33.560
We need every vote.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.790
We've got to run up the vote in the blue states.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:40.890
We've got to get as many possible votes in
red states, even if they are states that Biden

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.820
will certainly lose.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:49.650
Don't assume anything whatsoever because gut
feeling about the economy is exactly who's

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.840
being targeted when Trump says biggest job
growth in one month ever will.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:55.840
Yeah.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.000
After record losses.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.000
Right.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.000
Those are the folks that go.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.000
I don't know.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.930
It just kind of sounds like things are pretty
good.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.140
The stock market looks OK.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:08.510
This is why they pour money into the stock
market to reach those exact voters.

